ter.\emdash  I don\rquote t understand! What do you [mean] (}{\i\f211 want to say}{\f208 )?\par 5 Paul.\emdash  The one that climbs the tree first is the squirrel (}{\i\f211 which first ascends onto the tree, it is the squirrel}{\f208 ).\par 6 Peter.\emdash  Good (}{\i\f211 Well}{\f208 )! You are clever. But you only said what [was the difference between] (}{\i\f211 was between}{\f208 ) a squirrel and a bicycle. But what (}{\i\f211 what on-the-other-hand}{\f208 ) about the family?\par 7 Paul.\emdash  They are fine! Thank you!\par 8.\emdash  The squirrel climbs up the tree (}{\i\f211 the squirrel ascends onto the tree}{\f208 ).\par 9.\emdash  The ape climbs down from the tree (}{\i\f211 the ape descends from the tree}{\f208 ).\par 10.\emdash  The squirrels climb up the trees.\par 11.\emdash  The apes descend from the trees.\par }{\f208\cf13 \par }{\f208 NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. Sc}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 sne}{\f208 , from }{\b\f210 sc}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 o}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , i}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 to know}{\f208 . For the part. }{\b\f210 -ne}{\f208 , cf. L 3, N 3.\par }{\b\f210 2. Quid}{\f208 : the interrogative adjective-pronoun has the following forms in the singular:\par \tab \tab \tab \tab }{\i\f211 masculine\tab feminine\tab neuter}{\f208 \par subject\tab \tab \tab }{\b\f210 quis\tab \tab quae\tab \tab quid\tab \tab }{\f208 : }{\i\f211 who/what?\par }{\f208 direct object\tab \tab \tab }{\b\f210 quem\tab \tab quam\tab \tab quid\tab \tab }{\f208 : }{\i\f211 whom/what?\par }{\f208 possessive phrase\tab \tab }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 jus\tab \tab c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 jus\tab \tab c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 jus\tab \tab }{\f208 : }{\i\f211 whose?\par }{\f208 indirect object\tab \tab }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 i\tab \tab c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 i\tab \tab c}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 i\tab \tab }{\f208 : }{\i\f211 to whom/what?\par }{\f208 adverbial phrase\tab \tab }{\b\f210 quo\tab \tab qu\u225\'87\tab \tab quo\tab \tab }{\f208 : }{\i\f211 by whom/what?}{\f208 \par It resembles oddly enough the relative adjective-pronoun which differs from it only by the forms }{\b\f210 qui}{\f208  and }{\b\f210 quod}{\f208  instead of }{\b\f210 quis}{\f208  and }{\b\f210 quid}{\f208 .\par As their names indicate, these words can be either pronouns as the relative pronoun }{\b\f210 qui}{\f208  in phrase 5, or adjectives:\par }{\b\f210 quae res?}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 what thing?}{\f208  (subject)\par }{\b\f210 quam rem?}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 what thing?}{\f208  (object)\par }{\b\f210 qu\u225\'87 r\u233\'8e?}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 by what thing?}{\f208  (why, how) (adverbial phrase)\par }{\b\f210 3. N}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 scio}{\f208  = }{\b\f210 non sc}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I do not know}{\f208 ; }{\b\f210 sc}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 o}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 I know}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 4. Inv}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nio}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , i}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 to find}{\f208  (active voice); }{\b\f210 inv}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nior}{\f208  (i}{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 , i}{\b\f210 ri}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 be found}{\f208  (passive voice).\par }{\b\f210 5. Sim}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 sis}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 sit}{\f208 : }{\i\f211 that I be}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 that you be}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 that it be}{\f208  (subj. of e}{\b\f210 sse}{\f208 ). }{\b\f210 Fut}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 rus}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 a}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 um}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 which will be}{\f208 , is the FUTURE participle of this same verb.\par }{\b\f210 6. Is\u8230\'c9 qui}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 that which}{\f208  (when }{\i\f211 that}{\f208  replaces a masculine noun); }{\b\f210 ea\u8230\'c9 quae}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 that which}{\f208  (when }{\i\f211 that}{\f208  refers to a feminine noun); }{\b\f210 id\u8230\'c9 quod}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 that which}{\f208  (when }{\i\f211 that}{\f208  refers to a neuter noun). These words almost always come in pairs, but their order is often reversed; e.g.: }{\b\f210 is est sci}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 rus, qui asc}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ndit in }{\f208 a}{\b\f210 rborem}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 7. Inter}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 sset}{\f208 : another subjunctive! }{\b\f210 Inter}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 sse}{\f208  is a compound of the verb e}{\b\f210 sse}{\f208 , a verb which we must begin knowing: Pres. ind.: }{\b\f210 sum}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 es}{\f208 , etc.; pres. subj.: }{\b\f210 sim}{\f208  (vid. sup. N 5); impf. subj.: e}{\b\f210 ssem}{\f208 , e}{\b\f210 sses}{\f208 , e}{\b\f210 sset}{\f208 , etc. }{\b\i\f212 Int}{\i\f211 e}{\b\i\f212 rsit}{\f208 , of P 1, is therefore the 3}{\f208\super rd}{\f208  pers. sg. of the pres. subj. of }{\b\i\f212 inter}{\i\f211 e}{\b\i\f212 sse}{\f208 . Let us not yet search for the rules of use of the subjunctive, but content ourselves with gradually gaining familiarity with its forms.\par \par }{\i\f211 Let you start to see the differences between the prepositions:\par }{\b\f210 a}{\i\f211  (}{\b\f210 ab}{\i\f211  in front of a vowel) which indicates distance, difference, or introduces the agent of a passive verb, if it represents a person (the one who actually performs the action);\par }{\b\f210 e}{\i\f211  (}{\b\f210 ex}{\i\f211  in front of a vowel) which indicates the exit;\par }{\b\f210 de}{\i\f211 , which indicates the source, the descent and means }{\f208 also}{\i\f211 : about. E.g.: }{\f208 \u171\'c7}{\b\f210 De b}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 llo G}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 llico}{\f208 \u187\'c8}{\i\f211 , about the Gallic War, is the title of  }{\f208 \u171\'c7The War of the Gauls\u187\'c8,}{\i\f211  by Julius Caesar.\par }{\f208 \par EXERCITATIO\par \par 1.\emdash  How\rquote s Victor doing (}{\i\f211 What about Victor}{\f208 )? \emdash  Victor is well.\par 2.\emdash  What about the horses? \emdash  They are well.\par 3.\emdash  Victor\rquote s grandmother climbs onto trees.\par 4.\emdash  Who is coming down from the second floor?\par 5.\emdash  No one is descending from the third floor.\par 6.\emdash  In the neighbouring wood, a squirrel is not found easily.\par 7.\emdash  I don\rquote t understand what you are answering (}{\i\f211 What you be answering, that I do not understand}{\f208 ).\par 8.\emdash  Man descends from the ape (}{\i\f211 Man leads his origin from the ape}{\f208 ).\par 9.\emdash  Who wrote this stupidity (}{\i\f211 stupid thing}{\f208 )?\par 10.\emdash  The journalist writes easily.\par \par N.B. \emdash  For the 2}{\f208\super nd}{\f208  wave: do not translate }{\i\f211 descends}{\f208  in P 8 by }{\b\f210 desc}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ndit}{\f208 , which would not be very correct for this figurative sense.\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\f208\cf13 \par }\pard \qj\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\i\f211 To end this lesson, let us see the different forms which the words }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ter b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nus}{\i\f211  }{\f208 (}{\i\f211 the good father}{\f208 ) }{\i\f211 can take depending on their role in the sentence:\par \par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab Singular\tab \tab Plural\par }{\f208 #1 subject\tab \tab \tab \tab }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 ter b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nus\tab \tab p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tres b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ni\par }{\f208 #2 direct object\tab \tab \tab }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 trem b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 num\tab p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tres b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nos\par }{\f208 #3 possessive phrase\tab \tab }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tris b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ni\tab \tab p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 trum bon}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 rum\par }{\f208 #4 indirect object\tab \tab \tab }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tri b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 no\tab \tab p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tribus b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nis\par }{\f208 #5 adverbial phrase\tab \tab \tab }{\b\f210 p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tre b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 no\tab \tab p}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 tribus b}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 nis\par }{\i\f211 \par Do not try to learn the table above by heart: simply know that the different words do not follow the same rules of change according to case, and that we have n